---
// The three items here used to be one-liners in Trajectory's "tooling and practice"
// list, where seven things of very different weight all got the same 90 characters.
// These three earned more room for the same reason the case studies did: each one is
// checkable in the repo, and the specifics are the whole argument. The other four stay
// in the list.
//
// Everything below was read out of the ezofficeinventory working tree rather than
// restated from the resume. Nothing here links out, because none of it is public, so
// the counts and file names are doing the work a proof link usually does. That is also
// why they have to stay exact: "12 rules and 3 skills" is checkable by anyone who can
// see the repo, and "a suite of coding-agent tooling" is not.
//
// Deliberately not cards. OpenSource sits directly below this and is already a card
// grid; two card sections in a row read as one long grid with a heading stuck in it.
// The role-node treatment ties it back to Trajectory above instead, which is the
// section it is the payoff for.
---

<section id="leverage" class="bg-ink border-t border-line">
  <div class="band shell">
    <div class="measure">
      <p class="marker mb-6">Internal tooling</p>
      <h2 class="t-display-2 text-paper">Tooling nobody had to opt into.</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-paper/75 leading-relaxed">
        Three things that ship with the work repo. None of them needed a rollout email.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="mt-12 max-w-[46rem] flex flex-col">
      <li data-reveal class="role-node py-7 border-t border-line">
        <h3 class="t-display-3 text-paper">Rules and skills that arrive with the branch</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-quiet">12 rules &middot; 3 skills &middot; merged June 2026</p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4 text-paper/75 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Cursor rules covering the Rails and React sides of the codebase, plus three skills that
            know the house conventions. <code>react-new-module</code> scaffolds a module the way this
            codebase builds them: screens, containers, hooks, Redux slices, API calls, parsers, routes,
            i18n. <code>custom-role-permissions</code> walks the YAML entry, the
            <code>allow_ez_*</code> method, the CanCan rule and the backfill rake task, which is four
            files in three directories if you are doing it from memory.
          </p>
          <p>
            They went in through an ordinary pull request. Every branch cut since has carried them, so
            there is nothing to install and nothing to keep in sync.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li data-reveal class="role-node py-7 border-t border-line">
        <h3 class="t-display-3 text-paper">Three hooks nobody installs</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-quiet">pre-commit &middot; pre-push &middot; prepare-commit-message</p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4 text-paper/75 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            The setup script for each of the four products calls the hook installer, so they land the
            first time an engineer runs it and quietly update themselves on every run after that.
          </p>
          <p>
            The pre-commit hook blocks what gets committed by accident: a leftover
            <code>binding.pry</code>, a conflict marker, a stray <code>console.log</code>, a
            <code>Gemfile</code> staged without its lockfile. It reads only newly added lines, so a
            debug statement that was already there does not block you from touching the file. The
            pre-push hook refuses Ruby syntax errors outright. The third reads the ticket number out
            of the branch name and appends it to the commit message, which is the kind of thing
            everyone agrees to do and then forgets by Thursday.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li data-reveal class="role-node py-7 border-t border-line">
        <h3 class="t-display-3 text-paper">An N+1 alert with a line number</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-quiet">Bullet &middot; UniformNotifier patch &middot; staging</p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4 text-paper/75 leading-relaxed">
          <p>Bullet already finds the N+1 queries. Getting anyone to read the log was the hard part.</p>
          <p>
            This prepends a module into <code>UniformNotifier::Slack</code> and rewrites what gets
            posted. The message carries who triggered it, which of the four products it came from, the
            request URL, Bullet's own suggested fix, and the top five stack frames. Then a link
            straight to the offending file and line on GitHub.
          </p>
          <p>
            It runs on staging only, which is the whole point. The regression surfaces on a shared
            environment rather than in production, and the person who caused it hears about it in a
            channel instead of a log file nobody opens.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <!-- Outside the list: an ol may only contain li. -->
    <div class="max-w-[46rem] border-t border-line"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The .prose code rule only applies inside a post body. Same treatment, scoped here,
     because these three paragraphs are the only place on the homepage that names
     identifiers. Kept at 0.875em so it sits on the body baseline rather than shrinking
     the line it sits in. */
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875em;
    background: var(--color-surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
</style>
